<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Mälaren</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mälaren</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Mälaren is the third-largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, stretching about 120 kilometres west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, where it drains into the Baltic. Its shores were the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigtuna">Sigtuna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> and later Stockholm, and its waters linked the region to Baltic and eastern trade routes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-malaren.jpg" alt="Lake Mälaren" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%A4larsee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
